data: {"kind": "text", "agent": "analyst", "text": "```sql\nSELECT\n    s.scene_number,\n    s.slugline,\n    s.summary,\n    sum(h.seeks) AS total_seeks\nFROM retention.seek_heatmap AS h\nJOIN retention.scenes AS s ON h.episode = s.episode AND h.to_bucket * 10 BETWEEN s.start_sec AND s.end_sec\nWHERE\n    h.episode = 4\n    AND h.backward = 1\n    AND h.to_bucket > 0\nGROUP BY\n    s.scene_number,\n    s.slugline,\n    s.summary\nORDER BY total_seeks DESC\nLIMIT 5\n```"}

data: {"kind": "text", "agent": "editor", "text": "In Episode 4, we're losing viewers during the long scene in the harbormaster's office explaining the dredging permits. This causes a 2.7% retention drop, which is a story problem, not a technical one. The issue is pacing: we hit the audience with June's huge emotional confession and then immediately pivot to a nine-minute administrative deep-dive, which breaks the momentum.\n\nThe information is clearly important, as this is also the most rewatched scene in the episode. The audience wants the clues, but the delivery is too dense. We could recut the scene, trimming it by three to four minutes to focus only on the most critical details of the permit history. Another option is to break up the exposition, intercutting it with Nora processing the fallout from June's confession to keep the emotional stakes high.\n\nThe rewatch data shows the audience loves moments of discovery. They're also replaying the dashcam footage reveal and Gil identifying the sheriff's truck. Let's use the dashcam clip of the two men at the water for the \"previously on\" to remind viewers of the core mystery.\n\nFirst, I'd recut the harbormaster scene. A tighter version will solve the pacing problem while still giving our core audience the details they're seeking."}

